{"task_id": "Mbpp/448", "solution": "_perrin_memo = {}\ndef cal_sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of Perrin numbers from P(0) up to and including P(n).\n    This function utilizes a helper to compute individual Perrin numbers efficiently.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _calculate_perrin_number(k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the k-th Perrin number P(k) using memoization.\n        Base cases are P(0)=3, P(1)=0, P(2)=2; recurrence is P(k) = P(k-2) + P(k-3) for k > 2.\n        \"\"\"\n        if k in _perrin_memo:\n            return _perrin_memo[k]\n\n        if k == 0:\n            result = 3\n        elif k == 1:\n            result = 0\n        elif k == 2:\n            result = 2\n        else:\n            result = _calculate_perrin_number(k - 2) + _calculate_perrin_number(k - 3)\n\n        _perrin_memo[k] = result\n        return result\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    # Clear the memoization for each new sum calculation to ensure correctness if called multiple times,\n    # though for a single call, this isn't strictly necessary.\n    # The design document implies a single-shot calculation without specific requirements on memo persistence.\n    # For this specific problem where it's called once, _perrin_memo could be global and persist.\n    # But if `cal_sum` is expected to provide independent calculations, it's better to clear.\n    # Given the problem context, it's a \"function to calculate\", implying a self-contained operation.\n    # Resetting it here makes it self-contained for each call to cal_sum.\n    _perrin_memo.clear()\n\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        total_sum += _calculate_perrin_number(i)\n    return total_sum"}
