{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple, Set, Any\ndef tuple_intersection(list_a: List[Tuple[Any, ...]], list_b: List[Tuple[Any, ...]]) -> Set[Tuple[Any, ...]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the tuple intersection of elements in the given tuple list irrespective of their order.\n    It normalizes tuples by sorting their elements before performing a set intersection.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _normalize_tuple(t: tuple) -> tuple:\n        \"\"\"Returns a sorted tuple of elements.\"\"\"\n        return tuple(sorted(t))\n\n    normalized_set_a = {_normalize_tuple(t) for t in list_a}\n    normalized_set_b = {_normalize_tuple(t) for t in list_b}\n\n    common_normalized_tuples = normalized_set_a.intersection(normalized_set_b)\n\n    return common_normalized_tuples"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/476", "solution": "def big_sum(array: list) -> (int or float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the sum of the largest and smallest values in a given array.\n    Assumes the input array contains numeric values.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not array:\n        return 0  # Or handle as an error, but per design, assume valid input.\n\n    smallest = array[0]\n    largest = array[0]\n\n    for num in array:\n        if num < smallest:\n            smallest = num\n        if num > largest:\n            largest = num\n            \n    return largest + smallest"}
