{"task_id": "Mbpp/599", "solution": "def sum_average(n: int) -> tuple[int, float]:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum and average of the first 'n' natural numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A positive integer representing the count of natural numbers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple containing the calculated sum (int) and average (float).\n    \"\"\"\n    sum_val = n * (n + 1) // 2\n    average_val = float(sum_val) / n\n    return (sum_val, average_val)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(arr1: list[int], arr2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays using a merge-like approach.\n    This function assumes valid inputs as per design context, including k being 1-indexed.\n    \"\"\"\n    len1 = len(arr1)\n    len2 = len(arr2)\n    p1 = 0\n    p2 = 0\n    count = 0\n    result = 0  # Placeholder, will be overwritten\n\n    # Merge elements from both arrays until k elements are counted\n    while p1 < len1 and p2 < len2:\n        count += 1\n        if arr1[p1] <= arr2[p2]:\n            result = arr1[p1]\n            p1 += 1\n        else:\n            result = arr2[p2]\n            p2 += 1\n        \n        if count == k:\n            return result\n\n    # If arr1 has remaining elements and k has not been reached\n    while p1 < len1:\n        count += 1\n        result = arr1[p1]\n        p1 += 1\n        if count == k:\n            return result\n\n    # If arr2 has remaining elements and k has not been reached\n    while p2 < len2:\n        count += 1\n        result = arr2[p2]\n        p2 += 1\n        if count == k:\n            return result\n    \n    # This part should ideally not be reached if k is always within bounds\n    return result"}
